**Premise correction:** There is no nightly database-cleanup job registered in this repository’s cron scheduler in production code. `internal/scheduler` exists as a reusable cron library, but nothing outside that package imports or starts it — only `internal/scheduler/example_test.go` and `internal/scheduler/integration_test.go` do.

The server’s background work is wired in `server/server.go` via `startBackgroundRunners`, which starts the S3 presign runner on a fixed interval (`server/runner/s3presign/runner.go`), not a cron-based database cleanup job.

The only “daily-cleanup” / “cleanup” cron entries are illustrative examples in scheduler docs and tests, not live jobs:

```14:21:internal/scheduler/doc.go
//	s.Register(&scheduler.Job{
//		Name:     "daily-cleanup",
//		Schedule: "0 2 * * *", // 2 AM daily
//		Handler: func(ctx context.Context) error {
//			// Your cleanup logic here
//			return nil
//		},
//	})
```

```97:106:internal/scheduler/example_test.go
	// Cleanup old data every night at 2 AM
	s.Register(&scheduler.Job{
		Name:     "cleanup",
		Schedule: "0 2 * * *",
		Tags:     []string{"maintenance"},
		Handler: func(_ context.Context) error {
			fmt.Println("Cleaning up old data...")
			return nil
		},
	})
```

Those examples use **`0 2 * * *`** (2:00 AM in the scheduler’s default timezone, UTC unless `WithTimezone` is set), but that schedule is not applied to any real database-cleanup handler in this repo. I cannot determine a production run time because no such job is registered.
